<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chieti</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Teate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chieti is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on a hill above the Pescara river near the Adriatic. As Teate it was the main town of the Italic Marrucini and later a Roman municipium, with temples, baths and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name gave the Theatine order its name, as its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iv">Gian Pietro Carafa</a>, later Pope Paul IV, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Chieti.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chieti.jpg" alt="Chieti" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ra Boe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chieti_2008_by-RaBoe_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
